The run validated the core approach (exact ℓ1 penalty + Adam + basin hopping found the right basin at 3.5s, 2.6310 → 2.63586 by 105s). The specific defect: **Adam leaves ~1e-4 of radius value on the table because the nonsmooth exact-penalty kink forces it to oscillate with amplitude ≈ final lr (~3e-6 per radius × 26 ≈ the entire remaining gap of 1.1e-4)**. Worse, that same ~1e-4 evaluation noise masks basin identity: a hop into a slightly better basin can score below the incumbent and get rejected — consistent with the trace going nearly flat for 400s (105.7s → 502.8s gained only 1.1e-5). Every candidate is being compared with ±1e-4 error.

Fix: exact evaluation. At fixed centres the radius problem is a small LP (max Σr s.t. r_i+r_j ≤ d_ij); solving its dual min ½‖1−Aᵀy‖² over y∈[0,1]ᴹ by Hildreth/Gauss–Seidel coordinate descent (proven convergent, M=351, sparse Gram ~51 nnz/row, warm-started) gives radii to ~1e-10 in milliseconds. The dual y also yields the exact gradient of the LP value w.r.t. centre positions (envelope theorem: ∂V/∂d_ij = y_ij), so `polishExact` does true gradient ascent on positions with an exact-LP inner solve and backtracking line search — the SLSQP-equivalent the harness notes demanded, replacing Adam's noisy polish at all decisive points (deep polish, periodic best polish, restarts, final climb). Safety properties unchanged: `best` is still only updated via `considerBest` → `scoreOf` (shrink-repair verified), and monotone line search cannot corrupt anything.

